{"id":"4e29019a-c867-47e0-b2fe-4d0f4723c7a7","object":"chat.completion","created":1784885614,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case.get('nodes', [])\\n    required_symbols = set(case.get('required_symbols', []))\\n    \\n    # Build maps\\n    node_by_id = {}\\n    children = {}  # parent_id -> list of child ids\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        node_by_id[nid] = node\\n        parent = node.get('parent')\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\\n    \\n    # Initialize present set\\n    present = set(node_by_id.keys())\\n    \\n    # Compute symbols present\\n    def symbols_present():\\n        s = set()\\n        for nid in present:\\n            s.add(node_by_id[nid]['symbol'])\\n        return s\\n    \\n    sym_present = symbols_present()\\n    \\n    # Order nodes: depth descending, then id ascending\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(node_by_id.values(), key=lambda x: (-x['depth'], x['id']))\\n    \\n    removed_order = []\\n    \\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in present:\\n            continue\\n        # Cannot remove root\\n        if node.get('parent') is None:\\n            continue\\n        # Cannot remove if not removable\\n        if not node.get('removable', False):\\n            continue\\n        # Cannot remove a required symbol\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        # Cannot remove if has retained children (children still present)\\n        child_list = children.get(nid, [])\\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in present for cid in child_list)\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        \\n        # Compute subtree (all descendants still present)\\n        subtree = {nid}\\n        stack = list(child_list)\\n        while stack:\\n            cid = stack.pop()\\n            if cid in present:\\n                subtree.add(cid)\\n                stack.extend(children.get(cid, []))\\n        \\n        # Check if any required symbol would be lost entirely after removal\\n        lost_symbols = set()\\n        for sid in subtree:\\n            lost_symbols.add(node_by_id[sid]['symbol'])\\n        # Remaining symbols after removal\\n        remaining = sym_present - lost_symbols\\n        if not required_symbols.issubset(remaining):\\n            continue\\n        \\n        # Perform removal\\n        for sid in subtree:\\n            present.discard(sid)\\n        sym_present = remaining\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n    \\n    retained = sorted(present)\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` for task SE-PE-01. The payload contains a list of nodes with properties: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols list. The objective is to reduce the tree by removing removable nodes while preserving all required symbols and keeping the root (depth 0) and nodes with retained children? Actually the description: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we need to decide which nodes to remove. The output must have removed_node_ids (the order of removal? \"actual removal order\") and retained_node_ids (sorted). The algorithm: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So we need to process nodes in order: first by depth descending (deepest first), then by node ID as tiebreaker. For each node, if it is removable (removable==true) and not root, and its symbol is not in required_symbols? Actually \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" meaning if node's symbol is required? But note: required_symbols list contains symbols like \"root\", \"s1\". So nodes with those symbols cannot be removed? But maybe also nodes that are ancestors of required symbols? The description: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That means after removal, the set of symbols present in the remaining tree must still include all required_symbols. Also \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So a node with retained children cannot be removed. Children are retained if they are not removed. So we process nodes in order, and for each node we consider removing it. But the removal order matters because removing a node might affect whether its descendants are still present? Actually if we remove a node, we remove its entire subtree? The problem says \"grammar-guided deletion and replacement candidates\" but likely it's about removing nodes (and their subtrees) from the tree. In Perses reduction, removing a node means deleting that node and its subtree. So we need to ensure that after removal, all required symbols are still present somewhere else. Also we cannot remove a node that has retained children because that would remove those children too, which might be needed. But the condition \"node with retained children\" means if any child is not removed (i.e., retained), then the parent cannot be removed because that would delete the child. But if we process depth-first from bottom, children might already be considered for removal before parent? Actually deepest first. So if a child is removable and we decide to remove it, then later when we consider the parent, that child is no longer there (removed). So then the parent might have no retained children and could be removed. So we need to simulate removal decisions one by one, updating the tree state.\n\nAlgorithm:\n- Create a set of node IDs that are currently present (initially all node IDs).\n- Create a mapping from node ID to node dict for quick access.\n- Create a mapping from parent ID to list of child IDs? We can build adjacency list.\n- Also track which symbols are present (set of symbols from nodes in tree).\n- Process nodes in order: sort by depth descending, then by node ID (string) ascending? The problem says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So we sort by depth descending, then node ID in ascending alphabetical order? Typically ID is a string like \"n1\", \"n2\", etc. Lexicographic order. That's fine.\n- For each node in that order:\n   - Check if node ID is still in the tree (present set).\n   - If not present, skip (already removed).\n   - If not removable (removable==false), skip.\n   - If node's parent is None or node is root? root id is \"root\". Skip root.\n   - If node's symbol is in required_symbols? The description says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\". So skip if symbol in required_symbols. But careful: required_symbols includes \"root\" and \"s1\". So nodes with symbol \"s1\" cannot be removed.\n   - Check if node has any children that are still present (retained children). If yes, skip.\n   - Tentatively remove: simulate removal of this node and its subtree (all descendants). Check if after removal the set of symbols still contains all required_symbols. Since we are removing the entire subtree, we need to check if any required_symbols are only present in the subtree being removed. But we also need to consider that some descendants might be removable but require further checks? However the condition is \"all required symbols remain represented\" in the whole tree after removal. So we need to compute the set of symbols that would be lost if we remove this node's subtree. That is: the symbols of all nodes in the subtree (including the node itself). If any required_symbol is only in that subtree (i.e., not present elsewhere in the tree outside the subtree), then removal is not allowed.\n   - If allowed, then remove the node and its subtree: remove all node IDs in that subtree from present set. Record the removed node ID (the node itself) in removed_node_ids list in order of removal. Also update the set of symbols present.\n   - Continue.\n\nAt the end, retained_node_ids are the node IDs that remain present, sorted (presumably ascending by ID string). The output requires removed_node_ids (order of removal) and retained_node_ids sorted.\n\nEdge cases: The payload also includes \"_case_nonce\" but we ignore.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Build adjacency: parent->list of children.\n- To find subtree of a node: use BFS/DFS from node, collecting all descendants. Precompute subtree nodes for each node? But as we remove nodes, the subtree may change because some descendants might have been removed earlier. However we process in depth order, so when we consider a parent, all its descendants have already been processed (since deeper). But we might have removed some of those descendants. So when we check \"node has retained children\", we need to check its direct children that are still present. Likewise, when checking if removal is allowed, the subtree is only those descendants that are still present. So we need to compute the current subtree from the node using present set. Since we process from bottom up, the subtree will be the set of nodes that are still present and are descendants. We can compute by traversing children recursively, only following present nodes. But to avoid repeated traversal, we can compute dynamically when considering each node. Since depth is limited (max depth 2 in examples), it's okay.\n\nWe also need to ensure we don't remove a node that is a required symbol. The root is also required (symbol \"root\" is in required_symbols). And other nodes like node with symbol \"s1\" are required. So skip those.\n\nWe need to return JSON object with removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nNow produce Python code.\n\nAlso note: The output schema requires additionalProperties false, so we must only have those two keys.\n\nWe'll write solve(case) that parses case, builds structures, executes algorithm, returns dict.\n\nWe must not mutate input case. We'll copy into local variables.\n\nLet's implement.\n\nPotential issue: The node IDs are strings like \"root\", \"n1\", etc. Sorting retained_node_ids lexicographically.\n\nThe removed_node_ids should be in the order they were removed (the order we processed and removed). The algorithm processes nodes in depth descending (deepest first), and within same depth by node ID ascending. If a node is removed, its ancestors later might become removable. That's fine.\n\nTest with provided fixture? Not required, but we can reason.\n\nFor first case:\nNodes: root(d0,removable false), n1(d1,rem false), n2(d1,rem true), n3(d1,rem false), n4(d2,rem true, parent n2), n5(d2,rem false, parent n3), n6(d2,rem true, parent n1). Required symbols: root, s1.\nSymbols: root(root), n1(s1), n2(s2), n3(s0), n4(s1), n5(s2), n6(s0). We need to keep root and s1 present. s1 appears in n1 and n4. We can remove n4 because s1 still present from n1. But n4 is deeper depth2. First process depth2: nodes: n4, n5, n6 (order by ID? n4, n5, n6). n4: removable true, not root, symbol s1 is required? Yes required, so skip. n5: removable false, skip. n6: removable true, symbol s0 not required. Does it have retained children? No children (depth2 leaf). Check if removal affects required symbols: subtree includes only s0. Required symbols root and s1 remain. So remove n6. removed_node_ids = [\"n6\"]. Remove n6 from present. depth1 nodes: n1, n2, n3 (order by ID? n1, n2, n3). n1: not removable (false), skip. n2: removable true, symbol s2 not required. Has retained children? n2's child is n4, still present? n4 is still present (we didn't remove n4). So has retained child, cannot remove. n3: not removable. Now depth0 root: skip. After processing all, retained nodes: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5 (n6 removed). retained_node_ids sorted: [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"]? Actually root is \"root\", sorted lexicographically: \"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\". That seems plausible.\n\nBut wait, is the algorithm correct? The description says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" For n4, symbol s1 required, but n1 also has s1, so removal of n4 would still keep s1. However n4 is not removable because its symbol is required? The description says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\". So we must not remove a node whose symbol is in required_symbols. So n4 should not be removed. That matches our code.\n\nThus output for first case: removed_node_ids: [\"n6\"], retained_node_ids: [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"].\n\nCheck second case: similar but different utilities. same structure. Same decision.\n\nThird case: same.\n\nThus implementation works.\n\nNow produce the JSON envelope with implementation key.\n\nWe'll generate Python source as a string with proper escapes.\n\nWe'll output the final JSON."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2172,"completion_tokens":2941,"total_tokens":5113,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2286},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":124},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}